WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:06.819
Magnesium is one of the lightest elements.
It’s right at the beginning of the period

00:00:06.819 --> 00:00:13.580
table and, in fact, it’s the first of the
light metals that you can actually use. Lithium

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:22.090
and sodium react violently with water so you
can’t really use them as a metal and beryllium

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:28.370
is fantastically poisonous so unless you are
using it in the lab where you can be sure

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:34.230
that nobody is going to touch it, it’s useless,
so magnesium is the lightest metal you can

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:43.359
use. So, for example, if you are trying to
make mobile phones that are really light,

00:00:43.359 --> 00:00:49.109
then you can make the frame of the mobile
phone out of magnesium. You can use it for

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:55.329
laptops. There is a professor in Germany,
who has promised to send me a laptop frame

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:00.050
made of magnesium, but it hasn’t arrived
yet.

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:05.770
So another classroom classic. Normally comes
along in chemistry lesson number three I guess,

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:12.000
time to combust a reactive metal in the air,
so the reactive metal we are going to look

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.000
at is magnesium.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.280
The other thing that is good about magnesium
is that there is a lot of magnesium about

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.229
in the world, so it is not an element that
is difficult to get. If you look at this periodic

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:34.969
table here, which is from Barcelona so it
is in Catalan, the areas of these, each element

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:41.170
are approximately the abundance in the world.
Some of the rarer elements have a slightly

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.479
bigger squares than they should do so you
can read the letters, but you see magnesium

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:51.329
here is pretty large, so magnesium and sodium
are pretty common, beryllium is much less

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:56.149
common, and lithium is even less common than
that, so there is lots of magnesium.

00:01:56.149 --> 00:02:01.100
Magnesium is very light, it is very reactive,
very pliable metal, it is very nice, it is

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.069
easily alloyed with things like aluminium
and other light metals to make very strong,

00:02:05.069 --> 00:02:10.320
very robust materials. Used in construction,
it’s used in high-performance components

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.610
of cars.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:19.450
When I was a child when we got, had indigestion,
we were given ‘Milk of Magnesia’. Magnesium

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:25.470
oxide in water which came in these really
nice blue bottles, sort of white looking liquid,

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.510
didn’t taste very nice, but it was meant
to be good for you, I am not sure if it was.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:38.330
It is used in the hulls of ships, you know
like, warships, things like that, so incredibly

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:44.080
strong, incredibly light but incredibly reactive
if you give it enough energy. So we are going

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.500
to combust it in the oxygen and see what happens.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:55.540
Magnesium as you know, burns, and one of the
ways that magnesium used to be used was for

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:05.740
photographic flashlights, and you use bulbs
like these which contain magnesium, inside

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:14.180
a bulb filled with oxygen. And for each photograph
you had to put this on the top of the camera.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:24.320
So I’ve got one here that I have taken out.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.841
So, if you look at it, so much heat has been
produced by this tiny amount of magnesium,

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.340
that it has melted the glass.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:40.900
So, let’s see what happens when we light
it. You can see, really fierce bright white

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.840
light that classical glow, everyone has seen
this in the chemistry lab at school, fantastic

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:46.840
reaction.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.260
It is one of the few metals which is meant
to burn in nitrogen. In theory you can take

00:03:52.260 --> 00:04:00.340
a piece of magnesium, set fire to it in air
so its burning well and then plunge it into

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:07.490
nitrogen gas, and it should continue burning.
I have tried this experiment lots of times

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:18.250
and it’s never worked. In fact for 25 years
I demonstrated this experiment to my lecture

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:26.170
class when I was talking about nitrogen, and
every time, it didn’t work, and the students

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:31.210
thought, the poor professor, he has been made
a fool of, it hasn’t worked and they didn’t

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:37.090
know that it never worked and I would have
been more surprised if it had done, but it

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.380
taught them a bit about the chemistry of nitrogen
and magnesium.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.130
Why did you keep doing it if it never worked?

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.180
Because it is a really good experiment, because
it got such a good reaction from the audience.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.390
Because it went wrong?

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.390
Yes!

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:53.580
You were playing the fool on purpose?

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.580
Yes!

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.930
So this is magnesium metal, but the magnesium
metal here is not the ribbon which we normally

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:04.180
see in perhaps, scientific labs or perhaps
in school labs, this is a very, very fine

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:08.250
powder of magnesium, so what we are going
to do in a moment is bring on a really very

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.620
powerful torch, a gas flame and we are going
to see what happens when we sprinkle a little

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:13.620
of this material into the flame.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:23.310
Magnesium is also very important in all our
lives, because magnesium is found in the green

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:30.860
pigments in the leaves of all plants, in chlorophyll.
Chlorophyll is an organic molecule, carbon,

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:39.350
hydrogen and nitrogen and in the middle there
is an atom of magnesium, and it is chlorophyll

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:45.440
which catches the sunlight and transfers it
eventually to cause the reaction of carbon

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:51.230
dioxide and water to make all the sugars and
other things in the plant. If that didn’t

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:57.820
happen none of us would be alive, because
we all rely either directly or indirectly

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:01.190
on plants, to give us the energy to live.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:06.260
I don’t understand completely the mechanism
of photosynthesis, because the energy that

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:11.700
is absorbed by one molecule, is transferred
to another and another one as it were along

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:15.950
a chain, but the magnesium is very important,
if you don’t have magnesium there it is

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:17.060
not nearly as efficient.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.490
Let’s light it again.

